connected to target database: XE (DBID=3062123222)

RMAN> # =====================================================================
2> # PROJECT 3 - PART 3 of 3: the disaster and the recovery
3> #
4> # Scenario: the datafile of the USERS tablespace is lost. That tablespace
5> # holds the KHADER schema, so this is a realistic "we lost the business
6> # data" event rather than a toy example. SYSTEM is deliberately NOT the
7> # victim, because losing SYSTEM takes the whole instance down and makes a
8> # much longer demonstration.
9> #
10> # The file is deleted at the OS level in 08_project3_disaster.sh, which
11> # is the actual "disaster". This file is the RECOVERY.
12> #
13> # Run with:   rman target /
14> # =====================================================================
15> 
16> # ---------------------------------------------------------------------
17> # 1. Confirm the damage. The datafile is gone from disk, so the instance
18> #    cannot open the file and reports it as needing recovery.
19> # ---------------------------------------------------------------------
20> SQL "ALTER SESSION SET nls_date_format = ''DD-MON-YYYY HH24:MI:SS''";
21> 
22> REPORT SCHEMA;
23> 
24> # ---------------------------------------------------------------------
25> # 2. Take the damaged datafile offline so the rest of the database can
26> #    keep running while we repair it.
27> # ---------------------------------------------------------------------
28> SQL "ALTER DATABASE DATAFILE 4 OFFLINE";
29> 
30> # ---------------------------------------------------------------------
31> # 3. RESTORE - copy the datafile back out of the backup set.
32> # ---------------------------------------------------------------------
33> RESTORE DATAFILE 4;
34> 
35> # ---------------------------------------------------------------------
36> # 4. RECOVER - apply the archived redo logs written since that backup,
37> #    so the restored file catches up with the rest of the database.
38> #    This is the step that ARCHIVELOG mode exists for.
39> # ---------------------------------------------------------------------
40> RECOVER DATAFILE 4;
41> 
42> # ---------------------------------------------------------------------
43> # 5. Bring it back online
44> # ---------------------------------------------------------------------
45> SQL "ALTER DATABASE DATAFILE 4 ONLINE";
46> 
47> EXIT;
